Stop an unreachable STUN server from pinning the GNS service thread.

CSteamNetworkingSocketsSTUNRequest::Think() treats a synchronously failed
BSendRawPacket() as "request over" and cancels it, and the
reflexive-keepalive callback answers that cancellation by immediately
issuing a new bind request to STUN server (idx + 1) % count -- the same
server again when only one is configured. Its first send fails
identically, so the cycle repeats with no delay whatsoever. On any host
that cannot route to the configured STUN server (offline machine, blocked
egress, CI sandbox) GNS reports it itself:

    Processed thinkers 10001 times -- probably one thinker keeps requesting an immediate wakeup call.
    SteamNetworkingSockets lock held for 24.2ms.  (Performance warning.)
        ServiceThread,CSteamNetworkingSocketsSTUNRequest::Think(x10000),SendUDPacket(x10000),SendBindRequest(x10000)

and nothing else -- ICE included -- makes progress behind it.

Throttled at the re-issue site, deliberately, rather than by keeping the
failed request alive across retries: an earlier version of this patch did
the latter and introduced a use-after-free (SIGSEGV in
STUNRequest::Think -> IBoundUDPSocket::GetRawSock on the service thread),
because a surviving request can outlive the socket it holds a raw pointer
to. Server-reflexive candidates are an optimization; delaying them a few
seconds is harmless, wedging the service thread is not.

